WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.497 --> 00:00:02.492
- You know, you might as well
shoot for something large,

00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:05.392
because you can still end
up with something smaller.

00:00:05.392 --> 00:00:07.663
But aiming for something
large is really important.

00:00:07.663 --> 00:00:09.555
When I went to all my smart
friends and asked them,

00:00:09.555 --> 00:00:11.920
basically 2/3 of them said I was an idiot.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.660
Entrepreneurship is a team sport

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:15.619
more than it is an individual sport.

00:00:15.619 --> 00:00:18.375
That if you're going to
actually pack up your wagons

00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:19.238
and go somewhere,

00:00:19.238 --> 00:00:20.612
makes sure that where you're going

00:00:20.612 --> 00:00:22.330
is potentially really good.

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:25.225
The number of companies that
really matter every year

00:00:25.225 --> 00:00:26.879
is probably between zero and five.

00:00:26.879 --> 00:00:28.507
Frequently entrepreneurs
say, "Oh, it's a secrete.

00:00:28.507 --> 00:00:30.359
"I have a hidden idea."

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:31.570
Go and talk to people about it.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:33.390
This is my main plan.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:34.478
If that's hitting problems,

00:00:34.478 --> 00:00:37.349
like I'm not growing fast
enough or I'm stalling out,

00:00:37.349 --> 00:00:39.763
here's some things I can try
to keep trying to do that.

00:00:39.763 --> 00:00:43.363
Every valuable start-up
has weeks and months

00:00:43.363 --> 00:00:45.034
of valley of the shadow,

00:00:45.034 --> 00:00:47.679
which is, why did I ever
think this was a good idea.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:51.023
We had this expression in
the valley called fail fast.

00:00:51.023 --> 00:00:52.883
Sometimes you find yourself, you know,

00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:55.836
pioneering, that you can't find
a way through the mountains.

00:00:55.836 --> 00:00:57.903
- He's an American internet entrepreneur,

00:00:57.903 --> 00:00:59.652
venture capitalist and author.

00:00:59.652 --> 00:01:02.365
He's the co-founder and
executive chairman of LinkedIn.

00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:05.832
He has an estimated net
worth of $4.6 billion.

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:06.857
He's Reid Hoffman,

00:01:06.857 --> 00:01:10.440
and here are his top
ten rules for success.

00:01:12.478 --> 00:01:14.486
- It's the same amount
of blood, sweat and tears

00:01:14.486 --> 00:01:15.794
when you start a company,

00:01:15.794 --> 00:01:20.454
whether or not it's kind
of a fixed vertical market

00:01:20.454 --> 00:01:23.215
or whether or not it's
something really large.

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:27.511
And the difference is that
you might as well shoot

00:01:27.511 --> 00:01:29.197
for something large because
you can still end up

00:01:29.197 --> 00:01:31.249
with something smaller.

00:01:31.249 --> 00:01:33.362
But aiming for something
large is really important.

00:01:33.362 --> 00:01:34.379
And part of the reason why this

00:01:34.379 --> 00:01:35.822
is kind of a rule of entrepreneurship,

00:01:35.822 --> 00:01:36.992
and maybe this is the kind of thing

00:01:36.992 --> 00:01:38.858
that this room is like, "Yeah,
yeah, we already know this,

00:01:38.858 --> 00:01:40.358
"we're living it."

00:01:41.384 --> 00:01:43.600
I hope. (laughs)

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.350
Is because if you don't start out

00:01:48.175 --> 00:01:49.658
aiming for the big game,

00:01:49.658 --> 00:01:52.136
you almost never can get there.

00:01:52.136 --> 00:01:56.653
It's got to be the kind of,
how do I have a global impact.

00:01:56.653 --> 00:01:59.403
I think all high impact companies

00:02:00.901 --> 00:02:04.735
are basically have to think
globally in nature these days

00:02:04.735 --> 00:02:08.590
because of the way that the
market ecosystem is going.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.980
And so you go, okay, how
do I play onto that stage.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:16.402
And part of that in terms
of the pioneer parallel

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:17.972
is, again, how do I go somewhere

00:02:17.972 --> 00:02:21.385
that is substantial and unique.

00:02:21.385 --> 00:02:23.529
For example, when I started
LinkedIn, I went to all my,

00:02:23.529 --> 00:02:24.901
because you should go to
all your smart friends

00:02:24.901 --> 00:02:25.996
and ask them.

00:02:25.996 --> 00:02:27.958
When I went to all my smart
friends and asked them,

00:02:27.958 --> 00:02:30.385
basically 2/3 of them
said that I was an idiot

00:02:30.385 --> 00:02:31.218
for starting LinkedIn.

00:02:31.218 --> 00:02:32.685
It was like, it was a
crazy idea that some.

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.750
That's a good signal.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.919
And the reason it's a good signal

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:37.672
is because if a 100% of
people think it's good,

00:02:37.672 --> 00:02:39.920
there's almost always problems
that you're not seeing,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.380
and that other people
think it's good, too,

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:44.877
that the competition piled
around it is serious.

00:02:44.877 --> 00:02:46.946
So I like the fact that you say,

00:02:46.946 --> 00:02:50.471
why would smart people think
this is not a good idea.

00:02:50.471 --> 00:02:51.729
But actually, in fact, there's something

00:02:51.729 --> 00:02:54.132
that makes it a good
idea even despite that.

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:55.839
Entrepreneurship is a team sport

00:02:55.839 --> 00:02:57.941
more than it is an individual sport.

00:02:57.941 --> 00:02:58.973
People obviously celebrate

00:02:58.973 --> 00:03:01.120
the individual heroic entrepreneur,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.503
and these stories tend
to be told in retrospect.

00:03:03.503 --> 00:03:06.510
But most successful
entrepreneurship projects

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.281
actually have two to three founders,

00:03:08.281 --> 00:03:09.606
sometimes even five.

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:10.810
Although two to three is standard.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:15.667
So make sure that you have
that solid initial founders,

00:03:15.667 --> 00:03:18.746
your co-founders, and the team around you.

00:03:18.746 --> 00:03:22.686
And your journey is almost
certainly going to lead

00:03:22.686 --> 00:03:25.231
you through what I call
the valley of the shadow.

00:03:25.231 --> 00:03:27.125
So that means that you should have

00:03:27.125 --> 00:03:28.634
a strong enough relationship
with your founders

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:31.934
that it will pressure
test in difficult times.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:33.642
Look for disruptive change.

00:03:33.642 --> 00:03:37.310
And the reason that I said
look for disruptive change

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:41.089
is because it's, what are the
opportunities that come about

00:03:41.089 --> 00:03:44.566
that are really new opportunities?

00:03:44.566 --> 00:03:46.043
Because the opportunity,
generally speaking,

00:03:46.043 --> 00:03:47.095
has to be large.

00:03:47.095 --> 00:03:48.194
It can't be something that's like,

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:51.778
well, it's been sitting
there for 10 or 20 years

00:03:51.778 --> 00:03:53.931
and no one's ever really thought about it.

00:03:53.931 --> 00:03:56.455
It's very rarely the right answer.

00:03:56.455 --> 00:03:59.785
And if it's not large
enough to be something

00:03:59.785 --> 00:04:03.702
that you can make something
significant out of,

00:04:04.856 --> 00:04:06.935
it's not really high impact.

00:04:06.935 --> 00:04:09.352
And the pioneer peril to this

00:04:11.425 --> 00:04:15.310
is that if you're going to
actually pack up your wagons

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.584
and go somewhere, make sure
that where you're going

00:04:17.584 --> 00:04:19.834
is potentially really good.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:25.426
And that the algorithm by
which you can think about that

00:04:25.426 --> 00:04:28.201
is all of a sudden
there's a new opportunity,

00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:29.803
it's a change in technology,

00:04:29.803 --> 00:04:32.077
it's a change in competitive landscape,

00:04:32.077 --> 00:04:33.722
it's a change in kind of how

00:04:33.722 --> 00:04:35.910
the global ecosystem comes together.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:38.757
The number of companies that
really matter every year

00:04:38.757 --> 00:04:41.200
is probably between zero and five,

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.832
in terms of the industry changing.

00:04:43.832 --> 00:04:45.265
So if you look at an audience like this,

00:04:45.265 --> 00:04:47.331
which is, what, 1400 people?

00:04:47.331 --> 00:04:48.844
1700 people?

00:04:48.844 --> 00:04:51.142
You say it's an average of
two founders per company,

00:04:51.142 --> 00:04:52.537
presume everyone's doing that.

00:04:52.537 --> 00:04:54.537
You got 850 in the room.

00:04:56.939 --> 00:04:58.113
And that's only the people who are here,

00:04:58.113 --> 00:04:59.284
let alone everything else.

00:04:59.284 --> 00:05:00.117
And so the question is,

00:05:00.117 --> 00:05:01.546
is how do you break through the noise

00:05:01.546 --> 00:05:03.106
is a really, really key thing,

00:05:03.106 --> 00:05:06.141
whether it's financing,
talent aggregation,

00:05:06.141 --> 00:05:08.471
go to market, et cetera.

00:05:08.471 --> 00:05:09.823
That, then becomes the central problem.

00:05:09.823 --> 00:05:11.986
So one of them is is to say, well,

00:05:11.986 --> 00:05:13.887
do something very contrarian.

00:05:13.887 --> 00:05:16.491
Do something that is not
like where people say,

00:05:16.491 --> 00:05:20.658
well, it's AirBnB but for
dogs, or that kind of thing,

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:26.684
is not the kind of thing to do generally.

00:05:26.684 --> 00:05:28.560
- It's very rare that that's
ever become the big company.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.393
- Yes, extremely rare.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.802
The second thing would
be is to look where,

00:05:33.802 --> 00:05:34.844
really look where,

00:05:34.844 --> 00:05:36.384
and this is one of the
reasons why both you and I

00:05:36.384 --> 00:05:38.247
are fans of Peter's question of

00:05:38.247 --> 00:05:40.141
what do you believe
and other people don't.

00:05:40.141 --> 00:05:43.712
But look where other people really aren't,

00:05:43.712 --> 00:05:46.776
whether it's hard science or

00:05:46.776 --> 00:05:50.757
if it was two and 1/2 years
ago, Bitcoin, et cetera.

00:05:50.757 --> 00:05:52.901
Those sorts of things where, actually,

00:05:52.901 --> 00:05:54.160
something could be really big here.

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:56.784
Because, by the way, if
you end up being right,

00:05:56.784 --> 00:05:59.664
you end up having access to the talent

00:05:59.664 --> 00:06:02.914
that's really focused on that.

00:06:02.914 --> 00:06:04.928
You have a ramp to actually
get a whole bunch of stuff

00:06:04.928 --> 00:06:06.519
and get a big head start

00:06:06.519 --> 00:06:08.774
from other possible competitive efforts.

00:06:08.774 --> 00:06:10.057
And these sorts of things.

00:06:10.057 --> 00:06:12.320
You want to get a good sense of

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.985
whether or not you're
on track or on target

00:06:14.985 --> 00:06:15.866
to where you're moving.

00:06:15.866 --> 00:06:19.563
So one of the things that
Chris, Ben and I write about

00:06:19.563 --> 00:06:21.512
in the Alliance, called
network intelligence,

00:06:21.512 --> 00:06:23.312
which is to get as much intelligence

00:06:23.312 --> 00:06:24.806
about your project as possible.

00:06:24.806 --> 00:06:26.272
So frequently entrepreneurs say,

00:06:26.272 --> 00:06:28.806
"Oh, it's a secret, I have a hidden idea."

00:06:28.806 --> 00:06:30.118
Go and talk to people about it.

00:06:30.118 --> 00:06:31.564
Get their feedback on it.

00:06:31.564 --> 00:06:33.522
Don't necessarily publish it to the web,

00:06:33.522 --> 00:06:35.091
but to go get that feedback

00:06:35.091 --> 00:06:36.965
in order to know how to adjust your plan,

00:06:36.965 --> 00:06:37.992
how to know what the market is,

00:06:37.992 --> 00:06:39.910
how to know what the weather looks like.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.215
These sorts of things in
order to be successful.

00:06:42.215 --> 00:06:44.517
Literally, anyone who is
smart who will talk to you,

00:06:44.517 --> 00:06:46.714
talk to them, get that feedback.

00:06:46.714 --> 00:06:49.214
Maintain flexible persistence.

00:06:50.174 --> 00:06:52.277
Entrepreneurs are given
two pieces of advice

00:06:52.277 --> 00:06:53.344
with equal vigor.

00:06:53.344 --> 00:06:56.705
If you look at them,
they're in contradiction.

00:06:56.705 --> 00:06:58.384
On is have a vision.

00:06:58.384 --> 00:06:59.294
You'll plow through the wall.

00:06:59.294 --> 00:07:00.186
Keep your vision.

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:02.465
Don't allow yourself to
be de-focused from that.

00:07:02.465 --> 00:07:03.553
Keep going.

00:07:03.553 --> 00:07:05.035
The other is, listen to your customers,

00:07:05.035 --> 00:07:07.606
listen to the market feedback,
listen to your network.

00:07:07.606 --> 00:07:09.329
Be really adaptive.

00:07:09.329 --> 00:07:13.705
You're like, well, okay. (laughs)

00:07:13.705 --> 00:07:15.320
How do you put those two things together?

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:17.615
And that's part of, again, where

00:07:17.615 --> 00:07:21.644
it's a very difficult thing
to teach, other than by doing,

00:07:21.644 --> 00:07:24.486
other than by the activity of going out

00:07:24.486 --> 00:07:28.494
and being an entrepreneur
and making those decisions.

00:07:28.494 --> 00:07:32.661
And so the reason the rule is
maintain flexible persistence

00:07:33.541 --> 00:07:38.005
is because it's both keep
a vision and be flexible.

00:07:38.005 --> 00:07:39.001
So, for example, if you think

00:07:39.001 --> 00:07:41.224
about the ABZ planning framework,

00:07:41.224 --> 00:07:42.536
it's like, look, I've got a plan,

00:07:42.536 --> 00:07:45.544
I want to try to get there,
this is my main plan.

00:07:45.544 --> 00:07:46.577
If that's hitting problems,

00:07:46.577 --> 00:07:49.232
like I'm not growing fast
enough or I'm stalling out,

00:07:49.232 --> 00:07:51.019
okay, here's some things I can try

00:07:51.019 --> 00:07:52.441
to keep trying to do that.

00:07:52.441 --> 00:07:53.992
And if that doesn't work, how do I shift

00:07:53.992 --> 00:07:56.305
to a different destination,
a different location?

00:07:56.305 --> 00:07:57.951
That's part of the plan Z.

00:07:57.951 --> 00:07:59.160
One of the paradoxes of entrepreneurship

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.180
is you do these kind of two things

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.518
that are in contradiction and balance.

00:08:03.518 --> 00:08:04.510
So one of them is you actually

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.119
want to have some self-delusion,

00:08:06.119 --> 00:08:07.854
like, yes, I'm sure I can make it work.

00:08:07.854 --> 00:08:09.557
On the other hand, you
want to be asking yourself

00:08:09.557 --> 00:08:11.382
every day and every week,

00:08:11.382 --> 00:08:13.766
is my confidence in this going up or down,

00:08:13.766 --> 00:08:15.587
and if it's not going up,

00:08:15.587 --> 00:08:17.314
what do I need to do and learn and pivot

00:08:17.314 --> 00:08:19.704
in order to have it?

00:08:19.704 --> 00:08:21.829
And it doesn't have to be
that it goes up every week.

00:08:21.829 --> 00:08:25.391
I mean PayPal, LinkedIn, we've had weeks.

00:08:25.391 --> 00:08:28.862
Every valuable start-up
has weeks and months

00:08:28.862 --> 00:08:31.566
of valley of the shadow which is,

00:08:31.566 --> 00:08:33.553
why did I ever think this was a good idea?

00:08:33.553 --> 00:08:35.547
And so that happens, so
the confidence can go down.

00:08:35.547 --> 00:08:37.367
And that's fine,

00:08:37.367 --> 00:08:39.339
but part of the reason
you're asking this question

00:08:39.339 --> 00:08:41.023
is in order to know what problem

00:08:41.023 --> 00:08:42.817
do I need to attack aggressively

00:08:42.817 --> 00:08:44.583
to think that I'm on the right path.

00:08:44.583 --> 00:08:48.031
We have this expression in
the valley called fail fast.

00:08:48.031 --> 00:08:51.060
Really what that is, is the measuring

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:52.860
the confidence interval,

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:54.311
the confidence in your investment thesis

00:08:54.311 --> 00:08:55.704
and what you're doing.

00:08:55.704 --> 00:08:56.819
And be measuring that in a way

00:08:56.819 --> 00:08:59.909
that when it begins decreasing,

00:08:59.909 --> 00:09:02.835
and decreases substantially,
again and again over time,

00:09:02.835 --> 00:09:05.000
to pivot, to change.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.423
And you want to be getting
as much early data,

00:09:07.423 --> 00:09:08.809
as much early feedback.

00:09:08.809 --> 00:09:11.074
It isn't even all sedated,
it could be perspective.

00:09:11.074 --> 00:09:13.691
To know whether or not your
plan is going to work or not

00:09:13.691 --> 00:09:15.264
so that you can adjust early.

00:09:15.264 --> 00:09:17.290
Because if you stay, oh,
I just have my vision,

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:19.363
and I'm going to work
on this for seven years,

00:09:19.363 --> 00:09:22.044
you could frequently be nowhere,

00:09:22.044 --> 00:09:26.180
where if you had taken
network intelligence

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:29.057
and you had adjusted your
plan as you're going,

00:09:29.057 --> 00:09:30.918
you could end up being very successful.

00:09:30.918 --> 00:09:33.691
A plan for both good and bad luck.

00:09:33.691 --> 00:09:35.305
And part of what it is is,

00:09:35.305 --> 00:09:36.833
people say, "What do you
mean plan for good luck?

00:09:36.833 --> 00:09:40.292
"I have this idea, and it works out

00:09:40.292 --> 00:09:42.626
"and then things keep going."

00:09:42.626 --> 00:09:46.543
Well, good luck actually
comes in opportunities

00:09:47.596 --> 00:09:49.929
that you don't normally see.

00:09:53.058 --> 00:09:55.558
So for example, PayPal started

00:09:56.534 --> 00:09:59.951
as a encryption on mobile phones company.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.325
Because it was too close friends of mine,

00:10:02.325 --> 00:10:04.072
I joined the board when they founded it.

00:10:04.072 --> 00:10:05.491
Even though I thought
encryption technology

00:10:05.491 --> 00:10:08.245
in mobile phones back in December 98

00:10:08.245 --> 00:10:11.495
was not a good idea, which I told them.

00:10:12.809 --> 00:10:15.649
But it was kind of, like,
you also back people.

00:10:15.649 --> 00:10:17.744
And then they were, like, okay,

00:10:17.744 --> 00:10:19.054
not encryption mobile phones,

00:10:19.054 --> 00:10:20.287
maybe cash and mobile phones,

00:10:20.287 --> 00:10:21.748
maybe not just cash and mobile phones,

00:10:21.748 --> 00:10:23.233
maybe cash on palm pilots,

00:10:23.233 --> 00:10:27.056
maybe cash on palm pilots and
a payment service to the web.

00:10:27.056 --> 00:10:29.105
And then they launched with that.

00:10:29.105 --> 00:10:31.020
And for the first week, they said,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:35.026
well, all the this growth
is coming through eBay,

00:10:35.026 --> 00:10:36.236
but that's not really what we planned.

00:10:36.236 --> 00:10:38.965
We planned on people
bringing their palm pilot

00:10:38.965 --> 00:10:41.101
around with them and exchanging money.

00:10:41.101 --> 00:10:42.144
These aren't our customers.

00:10:42.144 --> 00:10:43.844
We should figure out how to get them off.

00:10:43.844 --> 00:10:47.196
And then, of course, part
of PayPal's great strength

00:10:47.196 --> 00:10:49.014
was being able to pivot fast,

00:10:49.014 --> 00:10:52.021
and went, oh, no, these
are our customers. (laughs)

00:10:52.021 --> 00:10:53.560
All the rest of that stuff doesn't work.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.302
This is the thing that we need to go to.

00:10:56.302 --> 00:10:58.708
And that's an instance of what I think of

00:10:58.708 --> 00:11:00.331
as planning for good luck,

00:11:00.331 --> 00:11:03.060
which is you basically you go,

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.343
okay, sometimes what happens
when you start the journey

00:11:05.343 --> 00:11:06.974
of going down a company is you're looking

00:11:06.974 --> 00:11:09.891
for an opportunity that you may not

00:11:11.322 --> 00:11:12.682
have thought of it when you started it,

00:11:12.682 --> 00:11:13.907
but then suddenly you kind of,

00:11:13.907 --> 00:11:16.024
like the pioneering parallel.

00:11:16.024 --> 00:11:17.955
You come around the
corner and all of sudden

00:11:17.955 --> 00:11:20.410
this new opportunity is available to you,

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:23.523
and that you head very strongly to that.

00:11:23.523 --> 00:11:26.107
And so good luck is not just,
oh look, my plan worked.

00:11:26.107 --> 00:11:29.963
It's, you will encounter opportunities

00:11:29.963 --> 00:11:31.552
that you will need to move quickly

00:11:31.552 --> 00:11:35.540
and grow into and exploit by
having been on the journey.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:37.804
Now, planning for bad luck is,

00:11:37.804 --> 00:11:39.541
unfortunately, a little
more straight forward,

00:11:39.541 --> 00:11:41.601
which is sometimes you find yourself,

00:11:41.601 --> 00:11:45.518
pioneering, you can't find
a way through the mountains.

00:11:45.518 --> 00:11:47.752
What's your plan B?

00:11:47.752 --> 00:11:49.684
And one of the ways that I
frequently think about this

00:11:49.684 --> 00:11:53.530
is I kind of have a plan
A, a plan B and a plan Z.

00:11:53.530 --> 00:11:56.165
The plan A is, okay, this is
what we're thinking of doing.

00:11:56.165 --> 00:11:58.609
Plan B is, well, if that's not working,

00:11:58.609 --> 00:12:00.591
what are my parameters of flexibility?

00:12:00.591 --> 00:12:03.286
I'm still trying to get to the same,

00:12:03.286 --> 00:12:06.906
build the same product, the
same product market fit.

00:12:06.906 --> 00:12:07.912
I'm still trying to get there,

00:12:07.912 --> 00:12:10.184
but maybe if I try this rather than that.

00:12:10.184 --> 00:12:11.017
And so, for example,

00:12:11.017 --> 00:12:13.184
when we launched LinkedIn,

00:12:14.705 --> 00:12:16.816
our hope was that people
would just invite each other,

00:12:16.816 --> 00:12:18.502
and the invitations would work,

00:12:18.502 --> 00:12:20.256
and it would grow in a big network,

00:12:20.256 --> 00:12:23.535
and that would be all we'd need to do

00:12:23.535 --> 00:12:25.339
in order to grow the network.

00:12:25.339 --> 00:12:27.331
Well, as it turned out,
we launched the network,

00:12:27.331 --> 00:12:29.857
and a couple weeks later we're growing

00:12:29.857 --> 00:12:31.809
at 2000 people a week,

00:12:31.809 --> 00:12:33.820
and it was, like, well,
that's not fast enough.

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:36.542
So what were the other
ideas we had in mind,

00:12:36.542 --> 00:12:37.424
the kind of plan B?

00:12:37.424 --> 00:12:39.174
And plan B was, well,

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.499
actually one of the questions
that people most have

00:12:43.499 --> 00:12:46.668
when they come to LinkedIn is
who else do I know who's here.

00:12:46.668 --> 00:12:48.623
So why don't we build
an address book utility

00:12:48.623 --> 00:12:50.193
that allows the people to say,

00:12:50.193 --> 00:12:52.323
how do I quickly find out who
else do I know who's here?

00:12:52.323 --> 00:12:54.681
Upload my address book, see who's here.

00:12:54.681 --> 00:12:56.405
We invented that whole pattern.

00:12:56.405 --> 00:12:57.984
And that caused our growth rate to go

00:12:57.984 --> 00:13:02.151
from 2000 a week to, I think
it was about 20,000 a week.

00:13:05.863 --> 00:13:07.549
Without that, I don't know
if we would have succeeded.

00:13:07.549 --> 00:13:09.454
Maybe other plan Bs would have worked.

00:13:09.454 --> 00:13:10.824
And then plan Z is what happens

00:13:10.824 --> 00:13:13.793
when it's just not working at all.

00:13:13.793 --> 00:13:16.242
It's kind of a life boat plan.

00:13:16.242 --> 00:13:18.904
And that's kind of the how you plan

00:13:18.904 --> 00:13:20.590
for both good and bad luck.

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:21.586
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:21.586 --> 00:13:24.327
I made this video because
Adam Jun asked me to.

00:13:24.327 --> 00:13:25.303
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:13:25.303 --> 00:13:26.954
that you want me to profile next,

00:13:26.954 --> 00:13:28.067
leave it in the comments below

00:13:28.067 --> 00:13:29.176
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:29.176 --> 00:13:31.063
I'd also love to know which
of Reid's top 10 rules

00:13:31.063 --> 00:13:32.357
hit you hardest.

00:13:32.357 --> 00:13:33.250
Leave it in the comments,

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:34.411
and I'm going to join the discussion.

00:13:34.411 --> 00:13:35.970
Thank you so much for watching.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:39.637
Continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

